# -*- mode: org; -*-

* 0.0.5-pre

** New features and UI changes

- gptel-agent now works over TRAMP.  This means it can run processes on
  remote servers, grep and edit files and so on without the need to
  install Emacs or an agent harness.  This involves calling command line
  programs on the remote, so it requires a POSIX-y remote environment.

- New user option ~gptel-agent-preset~ to specify additional gptel
  settings for sub-agents.  This is a preset that applies to all
  sub-agent calls, before any agent-specific settings.

  Its main purpose is to allow using a cheaper/smaller (or larger!)
  model for sub-agent tasks, which is a common need depending on the
  estimated difficulty of such tasks.  Of course, this is a gptel preset
  so you are not restricted to specifying a backend/model -- any gptel
  setting is supported.

  It can be set to a preset name (a symbol) or a plist spec, for example:
  #+begin_src emacs-lisp
  (gptel-make-preset 'lite              ;elsewhere
    :backend "Gemini"
    :model 'gemini-flash-lite-latest)

  ;; Apply the "lite" preset to sub-agent calls
  (setq gptel-agent-preset 'lite)
  #+end_src
  or just
  #+begin_src emacs-lisp
  (setq gptel-agent-preset
        '(:backend "Gemini" :model gemini-flash-lite-latest))
  #+end_src

- New command ~gptel-agent-compact~ to compact/summarize a conversation
  and free up the context window.  It works on the buffer up to the
  cursor position or on the active region, so you can compact a part of
  a conversation if desired.  Currently this must be invoked manually,
  but auto-compaction is planned for the future.

  Be warned: this is lossy and modifies the chat buffer!

  The prompt used for compaction is customizable, see
  ~gptel-agent-compact-prompt~.  You can also specify additional
  compaction instructions interactively by calling ~gptel-agent-compact~
  with a prefix argument.

  This command is experimental, and the implementation of prompt
  compaction might change in the future.

** Notable bug fixes

- ~WebFetch~ now handles textual responses according to their content
  type instead of sending JSON, source files and plain text through the
  HTML readability parser.  HTML extraction falls back to a full-page
  render or the decoded response body, HTTP failures include status and
  response excerpts, and oversized output is bounded with an explicit
  truncation notice.

- Restored ~gptel-agent-preset~ application for sub-agent requests.  The
  option is read from the owning parent buffer and applied independently
  before per-agent settings, preserving named-preset parents,
  composition forms and pre/post hooks.  Unknown named presets now
  report an error instead of being silently ignored.

- Sub-agents now receive an explicit completion contract: negative and
  inconclusive findings are valid outcomes and must include attempts,
  limitations and confidence.  Repeated/cyclic calls and exhausted web
  budgets are blocked with actionable feedback but no longer terminate
  the run, leaving the model able to return its supported or
  inconclusive result.  The overall round limit remains a final fallback.

- ~WebSearch~ now defaults to 10 results, supports a configurable bound
  with ~gptel-agent-web-search-default-count~ and
  ~gptel-agent-web-search-max-count~, and returns query/count/coverage
  metadata.  Its instructions discourage identical retries and direct
  agents to fetch URLs derived from structured identifiers directly.

